course: dictionary
lesson: 8336
title: Romanian: a place, a person or a language
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Romanian is one word with three jobs. As an adjective, it describes something from Romania, as in «the Romanian language». As a noun, it can name a person from Romania, or the language that people speak there. Check the words around it to see which job it has.
explain: «Latin was the language of Rome; French, Spanish, Italian, Portuguese and Romanian grew from it.» Here Romanian is a language, not a person, because it is one of the languages that grew from Latin. Try your meaning in the sentence: Romanian grew from it makes sense, so it names a language.
explain: Sometimes the text explains a word for you. In a family chat, a Romanian word may be followed by its meaning in brackets or after the word «is». Then you do not have to guess, because the clue is right there in the sentence.
fact: Romania is a country in south-eastern Europe.
fact: Its capital is Bucharest, and it has the Carpathian mountains and the Danube delta.
fact: Romanian is a Romance language, so it grew out of Latin, the language of the Roman Empire.
fact: «Spanish, Portuguese, French, Italian and Romanian all grew out of Latin.» Romanian is a sister language to these.
fact: Many Romanian families live in the UK, Ireland and Canada.
exercise: What is the capital city of Romania? | *Bucharest | Sofia | Budapest | Rome
exercise: «Spanish, Portuguese, French, Italian and Romanian all grew out of Latin.» What did Romanian grow out of? | Greek | *Latin | Arabic | English
exercise: Mum says, «We wanted more babies, but it didn't happen, puiule.» What does «puiule» mean? | Goodbye | *A pet name meaning little chick | A kind of Romanian bread | A place in Transylvania
test: Which word is the name of the language spoken in Romania? | *Romanian | Russian | Latin | Roman
test: The Dracula legend is set in which region of Romania? | Dublin | Ontario | Bucharest | *Transylvania
exam: Which language grew out of Latin, like Romanian? | Japanese | German | *Spanish | Chinese
